As with anything; that's why "practice" is precisely
what you need to do.

> My pets always seem to fall through trap doors, or
> get hit by falling rocks, or some such.

In the case of trapdoors, if I've already found and
descended, re-ascended the stairs, and especially if
I have only one pet, and I see the trapdoor the pet
fell through, I usually just jump down the trapdoor
(or go downstairs if the stairs are close) to
recover my pet, find the pet, go to or find the
upstairs, and return with the pet to finish
exploring the level.

Pets are _that_ important to the protection racket
player.

        [There is no guarantee that I'll land the PC
        on the same level as the pet just achieved,
        trapdoors can go down one _or_ several
        levels, and, I'm guessing, the pet may not
        get the same level out of the dice roll that
        the PC does, but for me this still seems to
        work "almost always"].

Sometimes I'll do that anyway, even if I _don't_
know my way up yet. It isn't like falling down
trapdoors is a novel experience for my PCs as a
group, anyway.

As for falling rocks, well, yes but...

If you linger on level one long enough for your pets
to achieve "cat", "dog" or "horse" status, they can
usually survive a single trap, and they heal fairly
well, though slower seemingly than the PC does.

As I've detailed elsewhere, you can effectively
share your PC's HPs with the pet by standing the PC
idly in reach of a monster the pet is also fighting.

The monster will divide its attention and attacks
randomly between the PC and the pet, while the pet
focuses its attacks fully on the monster.

Remember to start dancing the PC around the pet and
away from the monster when the PC's HPs get low,
though!

If you keep treats and food and fresh corpses
(snatched from the kills of existing pets if you
aren't hungry for them yourself) for taming pets,
your PC can usually enter mines with several pets,
ifyou explore at least the first four main dungeon
levels before starting down into mines.

At that point, losing one pet is saddening, but not
usually fatal to the PC or to plans to run the
protection racket.

IIRC, potential pets aren't generated as random
monsters for XL:1 DLVL:1, which is part of why
exploring the main dungeon a bit is a prerequisite
to turning "pet" into "pets".

And always, in the worst of circumstances, if the
pets all go missing, and monsters have you cornered,
the protection racket _is_ _optional_.

Just don't give up on it until no other choice
exists, which is usually a lot further on than first
impulse would suggest.

The habit of searching carefully for _all_ other
options than battle, even in such dire straits, is
one of the habits you _want_ to learn from
practicing the protection racket, but ultimately, it
doesn't _commit_ you to the protection racket if the
only other choice found is the PC's death.

Quantum valeat.

